.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt ZMETAD 8
.Os
.
.Sh NAME
.Nm zmetad
.Nd ZFS Metadata Export Daemon
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl fhv
.Op Fl d Ar database
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl r Ar days
.Op Fl -schema Ar file
.Op Fl -purge Ar dataset
.Op Fl -export-schema Ar file
.Op Fl -check-schema Ar file
.Op Fl -force
.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon monitors ZFS datasets with file-level event logging enabled and
exports their events to an external SQLite database.
This provides several benefits over the in-kernel ring buffer:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent
.It
SQL-based querying for time ranges, paths, and object IDs
.It
Configurable retention independent of pool space
.It
Integration with external monitoring and analysis tools
.It
Event persistence across pool export/import cycles
.El
.Pp
The daemon periodically polls all mounted datasets that have the
.Sy events
property enabled, retrieves new events using
.Xr zfs-events 8 ,
and inserts them into the SQLite database.
A sync state table tracks the last retrieved offset for each dataset
to avoid duplicate insertions.
.Pp
Database event history lags filesystem activity by at most one poll
interval (default 30 seconds).
Send
.Dv SIGUSR1
to the daemon for an immediate collection cycle.
.Pp
All configuration is provided on the command line;
.Nm
has no configuration file.
.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--export-schema file"
.It Fl d Ar database
Path to the SQLite database file.
The database will be created if it does not exist.
Default:
.Pa /var/lib/zfs/zmetad.db .
.It Fl f
Run in foreground mode.
By default,
.Nm
detaches from the terminal and runs as a daemon.
In foreground mode, log messages are written to standard output.
.It Fl h
Display usage information and exit.
.It Fl i Ar interval
Poll interval in seconds.
The daemon will check for new events on all datasets at this interval.
Default: 30 seconds.
Minimum: 1 second.
.It Fl r Ar days
Event retention period in days, 1 to 36500.
Non-numeric, zero, and negative values are rejected.
Events older than this are automatically deleted during the daily
cleanup cycle.
Retention deletes
.Sy events
rows only: the
.Sy gaps ,
.Sy sync_state ,
and
.Sy datasets
tables are never touched by retention, which is what makes the
.Sy gaps
counts lifetime figures.
Rows with a NULL
.Sy captured_at
(pre-v4 rows that predate ingest-time stamping) are never deleted by
retention.
Default: 90 days.
.It Fl -schema Ar file
Load the wire event schema from
.Ar file
instead of the schema embedded in the binary.
The daemon refuses to start if the file fails to parse.  A database
whose stored
.Sy schema_version
is NEWER than the loaded schema is also refused; an older stored
version is accepted, because record schema versions evolve additively.
.It Fl -purge Ar dataset
One-shot purge mode: instead of running the daemon, delete all
database rows belonging to
.Ar dataset
(the
.Sy events ,
.Sy gaps ,
.Sy sync_state ,
and
.Sy objmap
tables), then clear the dataset's in-kernel event ring, and exit.
This is the only mechanism that removes
.Sy gaps
rows; once purged, the dataset's loss history in the database is gone.
The kernel-ring clear preserves ring identity, and the dataset's
.Sy datasets
mapping row is left in place.
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 on a database or kernel error, and 2
if the dataset does not exist.
.It Fl -export-schema Ar file
One-shot mode: write the embedded canonical event schema JSON to
.Ar file
and exit.
Refuses to overwrite an existing file unless
.Fl -force
is given (the file is opened exclusively).
.It Fl -check-schema Ar file
One-shot mode: validate
.Ar file
against the embedded canonical schema and exit.
The comparison covers the full field set - field names, types, and
order - not just the schema version; any drift is reported and the
exit status is 1.
.It Fl -force
Allow
.Fl -export-schema
to overwrite an existing file.
It takes no argument.
.It Fl v
Verbose mode.
May be specified multiple times for increased verbosity.
.El
.
.Sh DATABASE SCHEMA
The SQLite database layout version is recorded in the
.Sy meta
table under the
.Sy db_schema_version
key; this build writes layout version 8.
The database contains six tables:
.Sy events ,
.Sy sync_state ,
.Sy datasets ,
.Sy gaps ,
.Sy objmap ,
and
.Sy meta .
The full column-by-column contract, including the
.Sy events
column list, loss-accounting formulas, ring-identity rules, and the
version-evolution and migration policy, is documented in
.Pa contrib/zmetad/SCHEMA.md ,
which is the stable consumer contract; the summaries below cover only
what a CLI user needs.
.Ss Sync State Table
.Bd -literal -offset indent
CREATE TABLE sync_state (
    dataset TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
    last_offset INTEGER NOT NULL,
    last_sync INTEGER NOT NULL,
    ring_guid INTEGER,
    last_lost INTEGER,
    root_id INTEGER
);
.Ed
.Pp
This table tracks the last successfully retrieved offset for each dataset
to ensure events are not duplicated after daemon restarts.
.Sy ring_guid
identifies the kernel event log instance the offset belongs to; it
changes only when the ring is replaced, and the daemon records the
replacement in the
.Sy gaps
table.
.Sy root_id
is the dataset root object id last reported by the kernel
(GET_EVENTS
.Sy root_objid ) ;
the full-path resolver uses it as the path terminus.
NULL or 0 means the root is unknown (legacy kernel reply), and the
resolver falls back to graph-emptiness heuristics.
.Ss Datasets Table
.Bd -literal -offset indent
CREATE TABLE datasets (
    dataset TEXT PRIMARY KEY,
    mountpoint TEXT NOT NULL,
    last_seen INTEGER
);
.Ed
.Pp
Maps each dataset to its mountpoint as observed at collect time.
.Sy last_seen
is the time of the collect cycle that last refreshed the row; rows
not refreshed within a cycle are pruned (one prune per collect
cycle), so the table tracks datasets the daemon currently sees.
Consumers resolve event paths against this table instead of calling
into the kernel.
The value is stored verbatim: mountpoints such as
.Sy none
or
.Sy legacy
appear as-is.
The table is neither deleted by retention nor by
.Fl -purge .
.Ss Gaps Table
.Bd -literal -offset indent
CREATE TABLE gaps (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    dataset TEXT NOT NULL,
    detected INTEGER NOT NULL,
    from_offset INTEGER,
    to_offset INTEGER,
    lost INTEGER NOT NULL
);
.Ed
.Pp
A permanent, per-dataset completeness record: one row per loss event
observed at poll time.
The
.Sy lost
column is a sentinel: a positive value is the exact number of records
lost since the previous poll; 0 marks a watermark regression with an
unknown count; -1 marks a ring replacement, meaning records before
that boundary belong to a different kernel event log.
Retention never deletes
.Sy gaps
rows, so they accumulate for the lifetime of the dataset in the
database; only
.Fl -purge
removes them.
Exact loss-accounting formulas are defined in
.Pa contrib/zmetad/SCHEMA.md .
.
.Sh QUERY EXAMPLES
The SQLite database can be queried directly using the
.Xr sqlite3 1
command-line tool or any SQLite client library.
The
.Sy captured_at
column holds the ingest time as unix seconds; the
.Sy timestamp
column holds the kernel's monotonic event time and is not a wall
clock.
The
.Sy path
and
.Sy old_path
columns hold dataset-relative names exactly as logged on the wire -
never join them with a mountpoint prefix in a WHERE clause; scope
queries by
.Sy dataset
and match the bare relative name.
.Ss Recent events across all datasets
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SELECT * FROM events
WHERE captured_at > strftime('%s', 'now', '-1 hour')
ORDER BY captured_at DESC;
.Ed
.Ss History of a specific file
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SELECT * FROM events
WHERE dataset = 'tank/data'
  AND (path = 'important.txt' OR old_path = 'important.txt')
ORDER BY txg;
.Ed
.Ss Files deleted in a dataset
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SELECT path, datetime(captured_at, 'unixepoch') as time
FROM events
WHERE dataset = 'tank/data'
  AND event_type = 'REMOVE'
ORDER BY captured_at DESC;
.Ed
.Ss Rename history
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SELECT old_path, path as new_path,
       datetime(captured_at, 'unixepoch') as time
FROM events
WHERE event_type = 'RENAME'
  AND dataset = 'tank/data';
.Ed
.Ss Events per day summary
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SELECT date(captured_at, 'unixepoch') as day,
       event_type,
       COUNT(*) as count
FROM events
GROUP BY day, event_type
ORDER BY day DESC;
.Ed
.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/lib/zfs/zmetad.db"
.It Pa /var/lib/zfs/zmetad.db
Default SQLite database location.
.It Pa contrib/zmetad/SCHEMA.md
Source-tree path to the consumer contract for the database schema
(all tables, column semantics, loss-accounting formulas, and the
version-evolution policy).
.El
.Pp
.Nm
writes no pidfile; the process is identified by the usual process
table or by its service manager.
.
.Sh SIGNALS
.Bl -tag -width "SIGTERM"
.It Sy SIGHUP
Reserved: accepted and ignored (configuration reload is not
implemented; there is no configuration file to reload).
The shipped
.Pa zmetad.service
unit defines an
.Sy ExecReload
line
.Pq Pa /bin/kill Fl HUP $MAINPID ,
so
.Ic systemctl reload zmetad
succeeds but is a no-op, consistent with this documentation.
.It Sy SIGUSR1
Request an immediate out-of-band collection cycle, independent of the
poll interval.
.It Sy SIGTERM , SIGINT
Gracefully terminate the daemon.
.El
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width "XX"
.It 0
Successful termination.
.It 1
An error occurred during initialization or operation.
.It 2
One-shot
.Fl -purge
mode: the specified dataset does not exist.
.El
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Ss Example 1: Starting the daemon in foreground mode
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zmetad -f -v
zmetad starting (poll=30s, retention=90d, db=/var/lib/zfs/zmetad.db)
Collected 15 events from tank/data
Collected 3 events from tank/home
\&...
.Ed
.Ss Example 2: Using a custom database and poll interval
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zmetad -d /data/zfs-events.db -i 60 -r 365
.Ed
.Ss Example 3: Running via systemd
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# systemctl enable --now zmetad.service
# systemctl status zmetad.service
.Ed
.Ss Example 4: Purging a dataset's stored events
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zmetad --purge tank/old
purged tank/old: 1234 events, 0 gaps removed; kernel ring cleared
.Ed
.Ss Example 5: Exporting and checking the wire schema
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# zmetad --export-schema events-schema.json
schema written to events-schema.json
# zmetad --export-schema events-schema.json
refusing to overwrite existing file events-schema.json (use --force)
# zmetad --export-schema --force events-schema.json
schema written to events-schema.json
# zmetad --check-schema events-schema.json
schema file OK (version 3)
.Ed
.
.Sh INTERFACE STABILITY
.Sy Evolving .
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Pa contrib/zmetad/SCHEMA.md ,
.Xr sqlite3 1 ,
.Xr zfs 8 ,
.Xr zfs-events 8 ,
.Xr zfsprops 7 ,
.Xr zpool-features 7
.
.Sh NOTES
The
.Nm
daemon requires that libsqlite3 be installed at build time.
If ZFS was built without SQLite support,
.Nm
will not be available.
.Pp
The daemon must run as root to access ZFS datasets and the kernel
event interface.
.Pp
The SQLite database uses WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) mode for improved
concurrent read performance.
This creates additional
.Pa .db-wal
and
.Pa .db-shm
files alongside the main database file.
.Pp
For datasets generating a high volume of events, consider:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
Reducing the poll interval to avoid ring buffer overflow
.It
Increasing the
.Sy events_size
property on the dataset
.It
Adjusting retention to manage database growth
.El
.
.Sh CAVEATS
The daemon exports events as they appear in the kernel ring buffer.
If events are overwritten in the ring buffer before the daemon polls,
they will be lost.
Such losses are recorded as rows in the
.Sy gaps
table (see
.Sx DATABASE SCHEMA
above), so the loss is at least visible in the database even though
the records themselves are unrecoverable.
For critical audit requirements, use a short poll interval or increase
the
.Sy events_size
property.
.Pp
The database sync state is based on byte offset in the ring buffer,
but offsets alone are not ambiguous across ring instances: each
sync-state row also stores the kernel ring's identity
.Pq Sy ring_guid .
If a dataset is destroyed and recreated with the same name, the daemon
detects the new ring automatically, records a
.Sy gaps
row with
.Sy lost No = -1
at the boundary, and restarts collection from offset 0.
No manual edits to
.Sy sync_state
are required or recommended; see section 6 (ring identity rules) of
.Pa contrib/zmetad/SCHEMA.md .
